#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# scientific_form

#% text
# `scientific_form(x)` shows the machine reals in `x` as `m × 10^e`, with six significant digits; `scientific_form(x, n)` with `n`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The argument is evaluated first; `scientific_form` stays around the value as a wrapper that changes only how it is shown.

#% item
# Only machine reals change: integers, fractions and symbols are shown as they are.

#% item
# Front ends show the form; text meant to be read back (`to_string`, scripts without display mode) keeps the wrapper.

#% item
# The pages keep values as text that reads back, so they show the wrapper; `mc-repl` and the notebook show `3.30809 × 10^5`, `1.23 × 10^5` and `[1.5, -2 × 10^-3, 2.5 × 10^7 + x]` for the examples below.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
scientific_form(330808.9917147705)
#% output
#= 3.30809 × 10^5

#% cell
scientific_form(123456.789, 3)
#% output
#= 1.23 × 10^5

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Every machine real inside an expression:

#% cell
scientific_form([1.5, -0.002, x + 2.5e7])
#% output
#= [1.5, -2 × 10^-3, 2.5 × 10^7 + x]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `number_form`, `input_form`, `n`
